# 4 — P0-A FORMAL RECOVERY REQUIREMENT

Define the exact recovery problem.

Scenario:

1. participant submits score mutation M;
2. canonical score commits;
3. client does not receive/retain final acknowledgement;
4. match/round later becomes Locked or Final;
5. scoring permission is revoked;
6. client must determine whether M committed.

Required:

participant may resolve the outcome of its own prior mutation;

participant may NOT submit a new score;

participant may NOT modify Official score;

participant may NOT regain general scoring permission;

participant may NOT inspect another participant's private mutation;

server must not weaken authorization globally.

Create:

docs/reliability/phase2c/POST-REVOCATION-RECOVERY-CONTRACT.md

---

# 29 — P0-B ACCEPTANCE — CONTINUED

If some derived work is intentionally owner-approved rather than automatic:

do NOT fail P0-B merely because it waits for owner approval.

Instead prove:

the score transaction creates/preserves all required canonical eligibility/state;

the owner-approved operation is visible;

the system clearly identifies it as pending owner action;

no automatic worker incorrectly publishes it;

failure/retry behavior after owner approval is durable and recoverable.

P0-B is about autonomous delivery for work that is SUPPOSED to be autonomous.

Do not accidentally automate financial/publication decisions.

---

# 154 — POST-REVOCATION GOLDEN TEST

Create one permanent golden regression:

1. participant is authorized;
2. participant submits mutation M;
3. M commits canonically;
4. response is lost;
5. match Finalizes;
6. permission is revoked;
7. same authenticated participant asks for status of M;
8. server returns COMMITTED;
9. participant attempts new mutation N;
10. N is DENIED.

This test represents the exact security/reliability distinction Phase 2C must establish.

Give it a stable Never Again ID.

---

# 155 — DERIVED-DELIVERY GOLDEN TEST

Create one permanent golden regression:

1. canonical score commits;
2. durable derived intent commits atomically;
3. worker is unavailable;
4. score remains Official;
5. worker returns;
6. intent is claimed;
7. derived calculation succeeds;
8. current pointer updates;
9. duplicate delivery occurs;
10. state remains idempotently correct.

Stable Never Again ID required.

---

# 220 — NO FALSE CLOSURE

A P0 is not closed because:

there is a workaround;

manual retry works;

Codex can repair it;

a report explains it;

a test passes only in source inspection;

a timeout was increased;

the failure is unlikely.

Closure requires the defined proof gate.

---

# 242 — SUCCESS CONDITION

Phase 2C succeeds when the remaining Phase 2 score-path P0s are closed at their appropriate non-Production proof layers and we have a candidate that can responsibly proceed to a SEPARATE staging-review decision.

The target outcome is not merely:

"tests pass."

The target is:

A COMMITTED SCORE CAN ALWAYS BE SAFELY RESOLVED BY ITS AUTHORIZED ORIGINATOR EVEN AFTER WRITE AUTHORITY CLOSES;

REQUIRED DERIVED WORK IS DURABLE, AUTONOMOUS, IDEMPOTENT AND RECOVERABLE;

DETERMINISTIC SQL DEFECTS ARE EXECUTED AND CAUGHT BEFORE RELEASE;

KNOWN WORKER DEADLOCKS ARE ELIMINATED OR SAFELY AND AUTOMATICALLY CONTAINED;

FINITE DATABASE TIMEOUTS FAIL ATOMICALLY AND RECOVERABLY;

CURRENT CLIENTS AND ROUND CONTROLS REMAIN COMPATIBLE;

AND NONE OF THESE IMPROVEMENTS REINTRODUCE HISTORY-SCALE WORK INTO CANONICAL SCORING.

---

